# kernel — the domain kernel threads and kernel-context work run in.
#
# Two findings from the 2026-08-10 permissive sweep land in this domain. One
# gets a rule. The other CANNOT get one, and saying so here is the point of the
# second half of this file.

##########################
# cpuset hotplug cannot evacuate tasks from an emptied cpuset.
#
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-10):
#   avc: denied { setsched } for comm="kworker/4:3"
#        scontext=u:r:kernel:s0 tcontext=u:r:system_app:s0
#        tclass=process  permissive=1                                    (x1)
#
# FACT, the source chain, all in kernel/m685/kernel/cpuset.c:
#   :292-293  static DECLARE_WORK(cpuset_hotplug_work, cpuset_hotplug_workfn)
#             - so it runs on a kworker, which is why scontext is kernel
#   :2242     cpuset_hotplug_workfn()
#   :2156     hotplug_update_tasks_legacy() -> remove_tasks_in_empty_cpuset(cs)
#   :2115     remove_tasks_in_empty_cpuset() -> cgroup_transfer_tasks()
#   :1410     cgroup_transfer_tasks() -> cpuset_can_attach()
#   :1441       ret = security_task_setscheduler(task);
#               if (ret) goto out_unlock;
# security_task_setscheduler() is selinux_task_setscheduler(), which checks
# PROCESS__SETSCHED from the CURRENT task (the kworker, i.e. kernel) to the
# task being moved (here system_app).
#
# WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT, under enforcing: the check returns -EACCES,
# cpuset_can_attach aborts at :1442, cgroup_transfer_tasks fails, and tasks left
# behind in a cpuset that hotplug has just emptied cannot be moved up to the
# parent. On this deca-core that is not hypothetical - sepolicy/toolbox.te and
# rootdir/m95-cpuset.sh document cpusets on this device collapsing to a single
# A53 under exactly this hotplug behaviour. This is the same pathology with the
# kernel's own escape hatch nailed shut, and unlike the cpuset decay it cannot
# be mitigated from userspace at all.
#
# ON THE TARGET SCOPE, honestly: `domain` is every domain, and that is wider
# than the one observation. It is still the right scope. The target is whichever
# task happened to be resident in the cpuset that hotplug emptied - system_app
# in this sample, any domain in the next one - so a per-domain list would be
# whack-a-mole that fails silently the first time it misses. What is kept narrow
# is everything else: one permission, one class, and a source domain that is the
# kernel itself. There is no platform neverallow on process:setsched (checked:
# no `neverallow ... setsched` anywhere in system/sepolicy public/ or private/).
allow kernel domain:process setsched;

##########################
# DELIBERATELY LEFT DENIED - and in one case, CANNOT be allowed.
#
# The Wi-Fi combo-chip NVRAM read, reported by both the connectivity and the
# telephony sweeps, fires as a PAIR of denials from the same comm in the same
# millisecond:
#   avc: denied { read open } for comm="mtk_wmtd"
#        path="/data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB/WIFI"
#        scontext=u:r:kernel:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:nvdata_file:s0
#        tclass=file  permissive=1
#   avc: denied { dac_override } for comm="mtk_wmtd"
#        scontext=u:r:kernel:s0 tcontext=u:r:kernel:s0
#        tclass=capability  permissive=1
#
# NO RULE IS WRITTEN HERE FOR EITHER, for two different reasons.
#
# 1. The dac_override half is NEVERALLOWED by the platform and would fail the
#    build. FACT, system/sepolicy/public/kernel.te, verbatim:
#      # the kernel should not be accessing files owned by other users.
#      # Instead of adding dac_{read_search,override}, fix the unix permissions
#      # on files being accessed.
#      neverallow kernel self:global_capability_class_set
#          { dac_override dac_read_search };
#    global_capability_class_set covers the `capability` class, so
#    `allow kernel self:capability dac_override;` is not a policy decision that
#    is available to make. Anyone who writes it will get a neverallow failure
#    out of checkpolicy, not a working Wi-Fi NVRAM read.
#
# 2. That matters for the OTHER half too, and this is the part worth carrying
#    forward: the connectivity sweep proposed `r_dir_file(kernel, nvdata_file)`
#    as the fix shape. If dac_override is genuinely required to reach the file
#    then that rule ALONE will not restore the read under enforcing - SELinux
#    would permit it and DAC would still refuse it. The two checks are
#    independent and only one of them is fixable in policy.
#
# The platform's own instruction is the way out: fix the unix permissions. The
# file is `-rw-rw---- root system` and mtk_wmtd is a kernel thread, which should
# not need dac_override for a root-owned file at all - so the interesting
# question is which component of the path lookup is actually short of DAC
# rights, and that has not been established. HYPOTHESIS: a directory mode
# somewhere in /data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB, or a kernel thread cred with a
# restricted capability set. FALSIFIER: `ls -ldZ` every component from /data
# down to the WIFI file and compare against the kernel thread's /proc/N/status
# CapEff. That needs root, which this pass did not take on a shared device.
#
# Owner: the connectivity lane (kalCfgDataRead16 / glLoadNvram, MAC + country
# code + TX power table + ucEnable5GBand). Left here rather than in
# connectivity.te only because the blocking constraint is a neverallow on the
# `kernel` domain, and this is that domain's file.

# RESOLVED — the falsifier above was run, and the answer unblocks the fix.
#
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-10):
#   /data/nvram, /data/nvram/APCFG, /data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB
#                                       drwxrwx--x root system
#   /data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB/WIFI       -rw-rw---- root system
#   mtk_wmtd pid 473:  Uid: 0 0 0 0     CapEff: 0000003fffffffff
#
# So the thread is root with the complete capability set, and every component
# of the path grants the owner what the read needs: rwx on each directory for
# traversal, rw on the file itself. DAC is satisfied end to end without
# dac_override.
#
# INFERENCE: the dac_override denial is SPURIOUS. The kernel probes
# capable(CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE) on some permission paths regardless of whether the
# access would already succeed, and SELinux audits the probe. It is logged, not
# required — which is why the read works today beyond the mere fact that we are
# permissive.
#
# That reverses the earlier conclusion. The SELinux half is NOT useless without
# the capability: allowing the file read alone does restore this under
# enforcing, because DAC was never the obstacle. Writing it.
#
# What breaks without it: wmt_ic_6628.c:1442 reads this file as
# WMT_CTRL_CRYSTAL_TRIMING_GET, and wlan/gen3 uses it as WIFI_NVRAM_FILE_NAME.
# Denied, the combo chip falls back to bIsNvramExist=FALSE with a zero crystal
# trim offset and the gen3 path loses the MAC and calibration blob — an
# untrimmed reference crystal and degraded RF, silently, at defaults.
allow kernel nvdata_file:dir search;
allow kernel nvdata_file:file r_file_perms;

##########################
# 2026-08-14: silence the spurious probe documented above. The RESOLVED
# analysis established mtk_wmtd is root with a full capability set and every
# path component grants the access without CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE — the kernel
# audits a capable() probe it does not need. The allow is neverallowed by the
# platform (public/kernel.te), and granting it is not needed; dontaudit is
# the correct remaining half so the 6-per-boot noise stops.
dontaudit kernel self:capability dac_override;
